<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Relax and Listen! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Can Connect with Nature!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Relax and Listen.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Develop simple responses to questions based on reading texts. Interactive: Participate in group-based brainstorming to draft ideas for a text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can develop simple responses to questions about nature from reading texts (e.g., "I see a tree."). Can participate in group-based brainstorming to draft and organize ideas for a nature text, using drawings and labels if needed (e.g., "The sun is bright.''). Can write simple sentences to describe abilities and observations related to nature using familiar vocabulary. (e.g., "I can see the sun." "I can't hear the wind.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large flashcards or images of various nature elements (tree, flower, bird, sun, stream, grass). Students quickly name the items they see. Teacher asks, "What can you see?" Students respond, e.g., "A tree!" "A bird!" Then, the teacher says, "Let's relax and listen to nature sounds." Play a short audio clip of nature sounds (birds chirping, water flowing) and ask, "What can you hear?" This activates prior vocabulary and connects to the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple picture of a nature scene (e.g., a park with a tree and a bird). The teacher points to elements and models writing simple sentences on the board: "I see a tree." "I see a bird." "The grass is green." "The sun is bright." Teacher explicitly points out the use of "I can see..." and the simple present tense for description, and pronounces /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun" clearly. Students repeat the sentences chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'I see a book' when looking at a tree?" (No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'green' a color for the grass?" (Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated narrative excerpt or descriptive text (1-2 short paragraphs) about a visit to a park or nature spot, similar to what students read in Lesson 2. Example text: "Today I am at the park. I can see a big green tree. A small bird is in the tree. The sun is bright in the sky. I can hear the water in the stream. I feel happy and peaceful here."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Expressing connection with nature: Talking about sensory observations and experiences in nature (e.g., "I hear the birds." "The flowers smell nice."). Locating people and things: Providing directions or identifying locations in nature (e.g., "I am near the big tree." "We are at the park entrance.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to corresponding images. Then, students answer simple written questions in groups on a mini-whiteboard or notebook, focusing on identifying key information for later writing:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What can I see?" (Students write words like: tree, bird, sun, sky.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is the bird?" (Students write: in the tree.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What can I hear?" (Students write: water in the stream.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher monitors and provides feedback on word choice for future writing tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple sentences using Can/Can't, Prepositions of place (in, on, near), and Present simple. Vocabulary: tree, grass, flower, bird, leaf, park, sun, sky, stream, water, walk, listen, look, relax, breathe, hear, see, quiet, peaceful, beautiful, green, bright.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences about a nature scene. They fill in the blanks using target vocabulary words from a word bank and appropriate grammar. For example: "I can ____ a yellow ____." (see, flower). "The ____ is green." (grass). "The bird is ____ the tree." (in). "I ____ hear the wind." (can't). Teacher circulates to provide support and immediate feedback on spelling and word choice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Picture Description Writing):</b> The teacher displays 2-3 different simple pictures of nature scenes. In pairs, students choose one picture. They brainstorm orally what they can see and hear, and where things are. Then, they *write* 2-3 simple sentences describing their chosen picture, using sentence starters provided by the teacher (e.g., "I can see...", "The ____ is...", "It is ____."). Example: "I can see a butterfly. It is near a flower. The flower is beautiful."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Correction:</b> Pairs exchange their written sentences from activity 2 with another pair. They read each other's sentences and use a simple checklist (e.g., "Did they use 'can' or 'can't'?", "Did they use a nature word?", "Is the sentence easy to understand?") to provide constructive feedback. Teacher guides the process, ensuring polite interaction ("Can you help me with this word, please?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided descriptive writing. Students will create a short written piece (3-5 sentences) about their "Favorite Nature Spot," incorporating elements of what they can see, hear, and where things are, and how they feel.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Group Brainstorming (5 min):</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher provides a large sheet of paper. Each group discusses their favorite imagined or real nature spot. They draw simple pictures and label them with words they remember (e.g., tree, river, sun, flower, bird). This visually helps organize ideas for writing. Teacher prompts: "What do you like about this spot?" "What can you see there?" "What can you hear?" "Where are you?" (Sociolinguistic: "This is my favorite spot in the park. It's peaceful.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Individual Drafting (15 min):</b> Each student takes their brainstormed ideas and uses a "My Favorite Nature Spot" template to write 3-5 simple sentences. The template includes lines and optional sentence starters like:
                        <br><i>"My favorite nature spot is the _______."</i>
                        <br><i>"I can see a big _______ and a small _______."</i>
                        <br><i>"The _______ is bright."</i>
                        <br><i>"I can hear _______."</i>
                        <br><i>"I am _______ the _______."</i>
                        <br><i>"It is very _______ and _______."</i>
                        <br>Students are encouraged to use vocabulary and grammar from the lesson (can/can't, prepositions, adjectives, present simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Sharing (5 min):</b> Students can share their completed written descriptions with a partner or read them aloud to their small group, focusing on conveying their ideas.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group brainstorming and a simple rubric for the written descriptive piece.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their individual written descriptions of "My Favorite Nature Spot."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Rubric - A1.1 Level):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Uses at least 3-4 target nature vocabulary words (e.g., tree, bird, sun, flower, grass).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Use:</b> Attempts to use "I can/can't" and/or simple present tense (e.g., "The sun is bright."). Attempts to use a preposition of place (e.g., "in," "near").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Cohesion:</b> Writes 3-5 simple sentences that are generally understandable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effort & Participation:</b> Actively participates in group brainstorming.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small paper slip before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can write about..." (e.g., what I see in nature).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used is..." (e.g., peaceful).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you like about writing about your favorite nature spot?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing today, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this writing lesson? Students were engaged during brainstorming, and the sentence starters were helpful. Most students could use 'can/can't' correctly. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with spelling and sentence structure beyond simple SVO. More visual aids for prepositions might be helpful. I need to reinforce blending of phonemes for common words like 'flower' and 'sun' when they are writing and sounding out words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their written descriptions as a basis for a 21st Century Skills Project, perhaps by sharing their "Favorite Nature Spot" with another classmate and mediating the information or creating a simple poster to display their writing and drawings. This lesson provided the core written output for that project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>